such that <MATH_0010>\nis the image of <MATH_0011>. Then\n<MATHSEG_0001>with<MATHSEG_0002>\nis smooth<FOOTNOTEOPEN_0001>Set theoretical remark: This coproduct is (isomorphic to)\nan object of <MATH_0012> as we have a bound on the index set\nby axiom [-1], see Sets, Lemma <REF_0002>.<FOOTNOTECLOSE_0001>.\nTo finish the proof it suffices to show this map is surjective,\nsee Criteria for Representability, Lemma <REF_0003>\n(this is where we use axiom [0]). By Criteria for Representability, Lemma\n<REF_0004>\nit suffices to show that\n<MATH_0013>\nis surjective for those <MATH_0014> where\n<MATH_0015> is an affine scheme locally of finite presentation\nover <MATH_0016>. By assumption (1) the fibre product\n<MATH_0017> is representable\nby an algebraic space <MATH_0018>. Then <MATH_0019> is smooth, hence the image is\nopen. Hence it suffices to show that the image of <MATH_0020> contains all\nfinite type points of <MATH_0021>, see\nMorphisms, Lemma <REF_0005>.\nLet <MATH_0022> be a finite type point. Then <MATH_0023> is\na finite type field over <MATH_0024>. Denote <MATH_0025>\nthe pullback of <MATH_0026> by <MATH_0027>. Then <MATH_0028> will give\na morphism <MATH_0029> whose composition with <MATH_0030>\nis <MATH_0031> and we win.\n", "source_text_hash": "sha256:6c574a163a09cf68a38f148b05ef2885bf7130b280f31331e384251755b34dcd", "unit_id": "tag:07Y4:proof"}
